<article class="env-exercise" id="02DO">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02DO">Exercise <span data-tag="02DO">111.16.1</span>.</a> <em>A silly argument using the complex numbers!</em> Let ${\mathbf C}$ be the complex number field. Let $V$ be a vector space over ${\mathbf C}$. The spectrum of a linear operator $T : V \to V$ is the set of complex numbers $\lambda \in {\mathbf C}$ such that the operator $T - \lambda \text{id}_ V$ is not invertible. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
Show that $\mathbf{C}(X)$ has uncountable dimension over ${\mathbf C}$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Show that any linear operator on $V$ has a nonempty spectrum if the dimension of $V$ is finite or countable. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Show that if a finitely generated ${\mathbf C}$-algebra $R$ is a field, then the map ${\mathbf C}\to R$ is an isomorphism. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Show that any maximal ideal ${\mathfrak m}$ of ${\mathbf C}[x_1, \ldots , x_ n]$ is of the form $(x_1-\alpha _1, \ldots , x_ n-\alpha _ n)$ for some $\alpha _ i \in {\mathbf C}$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>